Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 108, "THE LAST GUARDIAN"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 15 script pages of a 50-minute episode, plus a tag that is the first three beats of the film's first present-day scene. The season finale: it converges on Kharesh and stops where the film starts. Canon this draft respects: Auren and Lyra do not meet or see one another (Lock 3), so the near miss in the departure hall is only a spike on Tuan's array; Auren has never seen the Citadel (Lock 4); Malachar still holds exactly two stones (Lock 1) and sends Vesper and Corvin to bring back any stone found without hurting anyone, which is what the film's sedation-dart raid and "you are not to harm the boy" require; Seraphine and Malachar do not meet (Lock 2); Torin is already at Kharesh when Lyra arrives, as the film's Hollis line "the funders sent you to watch" implies. The twelve-ringed sigil and Ithren's note ("If they wake, the houses must prepare") are written to be the seed of the film's closing line ("The guardians have awakened. The houses must prepare.") and name no sign: the film's twelve signs are not shown here. Neither of the two clay vessels (101, 102) is opened; the film does not use them, and Isolde's rule "Two hands, never one" is kept. The exact phrase "Hold the other end" is not spoken (it is held for episode 107). Tag dialogue is reproduced from the film's first scene, ACT_1.fountain, and must be kept in step with it. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — DUSK

A vast bowl of red rock under a hard sky, the wind rising out of it like breath. No tents yet. A jeep on the rim and a long dry road behind it.

TUAN PHAM, a geophysicist, thirty-something, wrinkled field shirt, headphones round his neck, sets his array down in the red dust a hundred metres from the jeep and kneels to switch it on. He puts the headphones on. His face changes by degrees.

He looks up at the basin. At the rim. At the empty rock.

TUAN
(quietly, to nobody)
Oh. You again.

In the headphones, and in the air, and in the rock, a very low note rises: steady, dry, patient.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — THE RECORDS ROOM — NIGHT

The round chamber of pale stone, the alcoves with their grey tablets, the blue lamps burning low. Seraphine stands in the middle of the floor in her long coat, her face tired and clear.

SERAPHINE
(to the room)
A Warden has the second door and knows what he is. Tell me what he can read.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Whatever the door opens. He has not used it.

SERAPHINE
Not yet.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(precisely)
Not yet.

She turns slowly on the spot. She has come to ask a plain question and is not going to stay with it. She looks at the tablet in its alcove, the woman of fifty in a travelling coat: ITHREN. HOLDS MEMORY. LAST SEEN: THE SEA ROAD.

SERAPHINE
(low)
You are warm.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(a pause that is almost unseemly)
That tablet has been warm since the season the coins began to sing in Port Vessa. I did not remark on it. I did not know that I should.

Seraphine crosses to the alcove. She lays her palm flat on the grey stone. It does not open. She waits. The blue lamps hang over her like held breath.

SERAPHINE
(to the Lamp, very quietly)
It wants two.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(slowly)
I have not been asked to be a hand in four thousand years. I will be the second.

The lamps lower and gather, all of them, to a single pale point of light at the alcove's edge. The tablet slides forward from the wall a hand's breadth. Behind it, in the stone, a hollow.

In the hollow, a thin disc of pale metal the size of a saucer. Seraphine lifts it out with both hands.

INSERT — THE DISC. A ring engraved with twelve smaller rings, evenly spaced, each carrying a mark she cannot read. Not Aurelian. Not any alphabet she has seen. Beneath it, scratched into a strip of foil with a stylus, in a hand she knows better than her own:

If they wake, the houses must prepare.

Seraphine reads it. She reads it again. She sits down on the floor of the Records Room in her coat, quite suddenly, as if her knees had been told.

SERAPHINE
(a whisper)
What houses?

THE LAMP (V.O.)
I do not say what I do not know.

SERAPHINE
(looking up at the light)
You did not know this was here.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(very low)
She hid it from me. I have counted that she did.

Seraphine looks at the twelve rings. Twelve, where there should have been nine.

SERAPHINE
(to the disc)
Ithren, what did you do?

The disc is cold and gives no answer. In the sand of the floor, around her, the faint pattern appears and goes: a ring of twelve, drawn and gone.

ACT TWO

INT. PORT VESSA INTERNATIONAL — THE DEPARTURES HALL — DAY

A bright echoing hall of check-in desks, a departures board that clacks, a queue snaking round a pillar. People with cases. Children with balloons. Under it all, so low you would never notice, a note.

By a pillar near the ticket counters stands Auren, in a city coat, with a museum lanyard still round his neck and a paper in his hand: a printout of a map. At the head of it, in a librarian's caption: KHARESH BASIN. Uninhabited.

NELL, at his elbow, stuffs a sandwich into his coat pocket without being asked.

NELL
(very quiet)
You don't know that it's real.

AUREN
(not looking at her)
I know it's a place. I know she wrote it under the rings. I know I hear it when I read the word.

NELL
That isn't a reason to get on a plane.

AUREN
(a faint smile)
No. It's a reason to buy a ticket.

He goes to the desk. Nell does not follow. A clerk takes his card; the machine prints. He holds the little rectangle of card as a man holds something that might bite.

On the far side of the hall, behind the pillar and a family with six suitcases and a trolley, a group of travellers in field jackets heads for the security lane. HOLLIS carries a crate. LYRA, her notebook under her arm, argues with the departures board. TUAN, last, carries a flat case and the array in a grey hard shell.

The line on the array's little screen jumps, one sharp spike, and settles. Tuan stops dead. He turns, scanning the hall.

TUAN
(under his breath)
That's the ridge hum. In a terminal.

LYRA
(not turning)
Tuan. Gate.

TUAN
(looking at nothing; the crowd; a pillar)
Hang on.

At the pillar, the person who has just left it is already walking toward the doors with his back to the room. A coat, a lanyard. The crowd closes behind him. Tuan looks at the empty pillar for a long moment, then follows his team through security.

EXT. PORT VESSA — THE SEA WALL — DAY

A long stone wall above a grey harbour, gulls, a ferry heaving off in the swell. Auren stands at the rail with the ticket in his fingers. A plane crosses the sky, high and slow. He watches it go.

He does not follow it. He looks at the ticket, then at the sea.

AUREN
(to the water)
Not yet.

He puts the ticket in his pocket, beside a coin that gives its quiet note. He keeps his hand there. Nell comes along the wall behind him, out of breath, with the sandwich, and does not say a word.

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — DAY

The same bowl of red rock, now with a camp: tents, a generator, grid-stakes. Lyra stands at the rim in a field jacket with her hand shading her eyes, taking in a bowl the size of a small town. Hollis directs the unloading. Tuan crouches at his array in the dust.

At the far side of the rim, with his back to them, in a grey coat that has never seen dirt, stands Torin. He is looking at the middle of the basin. One hand is in his pocket, shut round an iron key.

LYRA
(across the camp)
You beat us here.

TORIN
(turning; courteous)
I wanted to see the ground.

LYRA
What does the ground say?

TORIN
(after a beat)
That someone has been very careful not to dig here for a very long time.

Tuan's screen, behind them, ticks once. Nobody notices but him.

ACT THREE

INT. A SANCTUM BENEATH A RUINED ABBEY — NIGHT

Stone vault. Candles. A long table. Seven niches in the wall: amber and brick-red filled, five empty. Vesper stands before the table in a travelling coat with a case at her feet. Corvin, behind her, tests the strap of a rucksack. They are dressed for a long drive.

Malachar stands before the niches, his back to them.

MALACHAR
(quietly)
Kharesh.

VESPER
A basin in the south. Nothing in it. Nobody has dug there for four thousand years.

MALACHAR
Somebody is about to.

VESPER
Dr Thorne's survey. Draven has gone ahead. Sir, we can reach them in four days.

MALACHAR
(turning at last)
You will go and you will watch. If a stone is found, you will bring it to me.

VESPER
(carefully)
Sir?

MALACHAR
And nobody is to be hurt. Not one hair. I would rather it were given. I will take it if I must, and I will not enjoy having had to.

CORVIN
(cheerfully)
And if somebody gets in the way?

MALACHAR
(gently; the full weight of him)
Then you are to be kind, Corvin. Please. It is the only thing I have left that I am still good at.

Corvin, abashed, nods. Vesper looks at her employer a long moment, with a face that has been thinking a long while and has not yet decided what to say.

VESPER
(low)
Sir. If the line is in that basin, and it is a person...

MALACHAR
(very quietly)
Then we will find out what Ithren left.

They go. The door closes. The sanctum is empty but for the man and the stones, five empty niches and the candles' small leaning flames. The note rises under the vault and holds.

Malachar takes from his breast pocket a square of paper. He folds it, unhurried, in the candlelight: one crease, another, a wing turned out. He sets the bird on the end of the long table, facing the wall, as one sets a place for a guest who is late.

He looks at the niches. At the five dark holes.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
(to the room; to the hum)
What are you waiting for?

The note holds. It does not answer. It never has.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

SUPER: "SIX WEEKS LATER."

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — DAWN

A vast bowl of red rock under a hard sky. Wind. Tents, generators, grid-stakes: an archaeological camp. In the centre of the basin, a square pit forty feet across, lined with scaffolding, descending into rock.

A drone buzzes over the pit, a camera on a tether. In the pit, a ramp, a door of black stone set into the rock, and, sprawled on the ramp with a laptop on her knee and a hard hat pushed back, DR. LYRA THORNE, sunburnt, hair knotted with a pencil, arguing with a screen.

LYRA
Tuan, tell me that's not an artefact.

TUAN PHAM (V.O.)
(over radio; tired, funny)
It's not an artefact. It's an anomaly in the data. Which I will remind you is a thing that happens when a pit has been dug for six weeks by people who want to find something.

LYRA
It's a pulse.

TUAN (V.O.)
It's a sixty-second repeating signal at nine point one seven one seven hertz. I looked it up. There's nothing in nature at nine point one seven one seven.

LYRA
So it's not natural.

TUAN (V.O.)
It's a refrigerator in your tent. Go look.

LYRA
(grinning)
I turned my refrigerator off at four.

A pause on the radio.

TUAN (V.O.)
(quietly)
Oh. Oh no.

On the ramp, behind Lyra, the black door has begun to hum.

CUT TO BLACK.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 108 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
